visor but um  now he has decided that this is the  moment to go and that is i think a very  strongly worded letter from a supposedly  mild mannered man he says that it would  make a mockery of the ministerial code  and worth saying i've also just been  given a response from boris johnson who  said that he is sorry to receive the  letter of resignation yesterday thanks  him for his service um he said that on  monday he'd said that he was content to  remain until the end of the year so this  letter has come as a surprise the prime  minister says and then he goes on at the  end to say that he's carried out his  duties  admirably but he also says that when he  says he was put in an impossible  position regarding my seeking of your  advice on potential future decisions  related to the trade remedies authority  so this is clearly um what this issue  has been about and worth saying this  morning dominic robb had said that there  had been a commercially sensitive matter  in the national interest and clearly  this has been the straw that has broken  the camel's back and led to lord geit  resigning  so we have the full letter i think from  what we've seen so far it is pretty  strongly worded clear  that this was him feeling he couldn't  carry on in his role given um what was  happening in government earlier today  william rag quoted oscar wilde saying  that to lose one ethics advisor might be  deemed a misfortune to lose two looks  like carelessness and i think um that  quote might be echoed over parliament  today mps i spoke to yesterday thought  that the timing and nature this short  statement meant that it wouldn't shift  the dial necessarily but this letter is  pretty strongly worded could this shift  the dial for some members of prime  minister boris johnson
 

